# A theremin synth

## Overview

Turn the screen into a theremin: slide a finger left and right to change
the pitch, up and down to change the volume. The `< sine >` arrows in
the top bar, or the volume buttons, switch the waveform.

Three parts work together:

* `Synth`, a process that computes the sound of 5 voices sample by
  sample and streams the mix into one long-running `aplay`,
* `Sequencer`, which records what you play into 4 tracks and plays them
  back on voices 1 to 4,
* a Scenic scene that turns touches into `Synth.set(:live, frequency, volume)`
  and draws the sequencer's buttons.

This builds on the [Audio](10_audio.livemd) and
[Screen, touch and buttons](11_screen_touch_buttons.livemd) notebooks.

## Route audio to the loudspeaker

Already done at boot on a Fairphone 3+. Run it if you switched to the
earpiece in the Audio notebook.

```elixir
for {name, value} <- [
      {"Earpiece Switch", :off},
      {"SLIMBUS_0_RX Audio Mixer MultiMedia1", :off},
      {"QUIN_MI2S_RX Audio Mixer MultiMedia1", :on},
      {"Speaker Switch", :on}
    ],
    do: {name, ExAudio.cset(name, value)}
```

## The synth

`aplay` reads raw 16-bit samples from its standard input and plays them
as they arrive, so no WAV files are involved. Every 5 ms the synth
works out how many samples the speaker will need by 30 ms from now and
writes exactly those. That keeps the delay between touch and sound
short, and the sound never falls behind.

Frequency and volume slide from their old to their new value across
each chunk, and the waveform's phase carries over between chunks.
Without that, every finger movement would click.

The synth has 5 voices: `:live` for the pad and `1` to `4` for the
sequencer's tracks. Each chunk adds the sounding voices up sample by
sample in one pass and skips silent ones, so an idle track costs almost
nothing.

```elixir
defmodule Synth do
  use GenServer

  @rate 48_000
  @tick_ms 5
  # How far ahead of the speaker we write: lower is snappier, higher survives hiccups.
  @lead div(@rate * 30, 1000)
  # The pad plays on :live, the sequencer's tracks on 1 to 4.
  @voices [:live, 1, 2, 3, 4]

  def start, do: GenServer.start(__MODULE__, nil, name: __MODULE__)
  def stop, do: GenServer.stop(__MODULE__)

  @doc "Play `freq` Hz at volume `amp` (0.0 to 1.0) on `voice`."
  def set(voice, freq, amp) when voice in @voices, do: GenServer.cast(__MODULE__, {:set, voice, freq, amp |> max(0.0) |> min(1.0)})
  def off(voice) when voice in @voices, do: GenServer.cast(__MODULE__, {:off, voice})
  def wave(voice, wave) when voice in @voices and wave in [:sine, :square, :saw], do: GenServer.cast(__MODULE__, {:wave, voice, wave})

  def voice, do: %{freq: 440.0, amp: 0.0, cur_freq: 440.0, cur_amp: 0.0, phase: 0.0, wave: :sine}
  def initial, do: Map.new(@voices, &{&1, voice()})

  @doc "Renders `n` samples of all voices mixed, as S16_LE, and returns them with the updated voices."
  def render(voices, n) do
    # Silent voices cost nothing. Each sounding voice becomes a tuple that is advanced once per sample:
    # {id, phase, phase step, change of the phase step, amp, change of amp, wave}.
    oscs =
      for {id, v} <- voices, v.amp != 0 or v.cur_amp != 0 do
        {id, v.phase, v.cur_freq / @rate, (v.freq - v.cur_freq) / (@rate * n), v.cur_amp, (v.amp - v.cur_amp) / n, v.wave}
      end

    {samples, oscs} = mix(oscs, n, [])
    phases = Map.new(oscs, fn {id, phase, _, _, _, _, _} -> {id, phase} end)
    voices = Map.new(voices, fn {id, v} -> {id, %{v | phase: Map.get(phases, id, v.phase), cur_freq: v.freq, cur_amp: v.amp}} end)
    {IO.iodata_to_binary(samples), voices}
  end

  # ponytail: one pass in plain Elixir, ~19 % of a core for 5 voices on the phone; Nx or a NIF if more voices are needed.
  defp mix(oscs, 0, acc), do: {Enum.reverse(acc), oscs}

  defp mix(oscs, left, acc) do
    {sum, oscs} = step(oscs, 0.0, [])
    sample = round((sum |> max(-1.0) |> min(1.0)) * 32_767)
    mix(oscs, left - 1, [<<sample::signed-little-16>> | acc])
  end

  defp step([], sum, oscs), do: {sum, oscs}

  defp step([{id, phase, dp, ddp, amp, damp, wave} | rest], sum, oscs) do
    next = phase + dp
    step(rest, sum + amp * shape(wave, phase), [{id, next - trunc(next), dp + ddp, ddp, amp + damp, damp, wave} | oscs])
  end

  defp shape(:sine, phase), do: :math.sin(2 * :math.pi() * phase)
  # Square and saw are much louder than a sine at the same amplitude.
  defp shape(:square, phase), do: if(phase < 0.5, do: 0.4, else: -0.4)
  defp shape(:saw, phase), do: 0.6 * (2 * phase - 1)

  @impl GenServer
  def init(nil) do
    args = ~w(-q -D plughw:0,0 -t raw -f S16_LE -r #{@rate} -c 1 --buffer-time=60000 --period-time=10000 -)
    port = Port.open({:spawn_executable, System.find_executable("aplay")}, [:binary, :exit_status, args: args])
    :timer.send_interval(@tick_ms, :tick)
    {:ok, %{voices: initial(), port: port, start: now(), written: 0}}
  end

  @impl GenServer
  def handle_cast({:set, voice, freq, amp}, s), do: {:noreply, update_voice(s, voice, &%{&1 | freq: freq, amp: amp})}
  def handle_cast({:off, voice}, s), do: {:noreply, update_voice(s, voice, &%{&1 | amp: 0.0})}
  def handle_cast({:wave, voice, wave}, s), do: {:noreply, update_voice(s, voice, &%{&1 | wave: wave})}

  defp update_voice(s, voice, fun), do: %{s | voices: Map.update!(s.voices, voice, fun)}

  @impl GenServer
  def handle_info(:tick, s) do
    due = div((now() - s.start) * @rate, 1_000_000) + @lead
    # After a long pause (busy CPU), skip ahead instead of piling up delay.
    written = max(s.written, due - 2 * @lead)
    {chunk, voices} = render(s.voices, due - written)
    Port.command(s.port, chunk)
    {:noreply, %{s | voices: voices, written: due}}
  end

  def handle_info({port, {:exit_status, code}}, %{port: port} = s), do: {:stop, {:aplay_exited, code}, s}

  defp now, do: System.monotonic_time(:microsecond)
end
```

A quick check of `render/2`: chunks join without a jump, silence renders
as zeros, two voices add up, and five loud voices are clamped instead of
wrapping around.

```elixir
# Check: Synth.render/2 joins chunks seamlessly, renders silence as zeros, and mixes voices.
one = fn wave, amp -> %{Synth.voice() | wave: wave, amp: amp, cur_amp: amp} end
silent = Synth.initial()
decode = fn bin -> for <<x::signed-little-16 <- bin>>, do: x end

# Two chunks of 100 samples join into exactly the same wave as one chunk of 200.
{a, s1} = Synth.render(%{silent | live: one.(:sine, 0.5)}, 100)
{b, _} = Synth.render(s1, 100)
{whole, _} = Synth.render(%{silent | live: one.(:sine, 0.5)}, 200)
true = byte_size(a) == 200
true = a <> b == whole

# Only silent voices: 100 samples of zero.
{zeros, _} = Synth.render(silent, 100)
true = zeros == <<0::size(1600)>>

# Two voices mix to the sum of each alone (±1 for rounding).
{live, _} = Synth.render(%{silent | live: one.(:saw, 0.5)}, 100)
{track, _} = Synth.render(%{silent | 1 => one.(:square, 0.5)}, 100)
{both, _} = Synth.render(%{silent | :live => one.(:saw, 0.5), 1 => one.(:square, 0.5)}, 100)
sums = Enum.zip_with(decode.(live), decode.(track), &(&1 + &2))
true = Enum.zip(sums, decode.(both)) |> Enum.all?(fn {sum, mixed} -> abs(sum - mixed) <= 1 end)

# Five loud voices clamp to full scale instead of wrapping around.
{loud, _} = Synth.render(Map.new(silent, fn {id, _} -> {id, one.(:square, 1.0)} end), 10)
true = Enum.all?(decode.(loud), &(abs(&1) == 32_767))
```

Start it and play a note for one second:

```elixir
{:ok, _} = Synth.start()
Synth.set(:live, 440, 0.3)
Process.sleep(1000)
Synth.off(:live)
```

## The sequencer

The sequencer has 4 tracks that share one loop length. A track stores
what you played as a list of `{time_ms, frequency, volume, waveform}`
events, not as sound. Every 10 ms it looks up, for each playing track,
the last event at or before the current loop position and sends it to
that track's voice in `Synth`, only when it changed.

Recording runs for exactly one loop from 0 s while the other tracks
with content play along, then stops by itself. A take replaces what was
on the track before.

```elixir
defmodule Sequencer do
  use GenServer

  @tick_ms 10
  @tracks [1, 2, 3, 4]

  def start, do: GenServer.start(__MODULE__, nil, name: __MODULE__)

  @doc "Loop length for all tracks, 1 to 30 s. Returns :busy while playing or recording."
  def set_length(seconds), do: GenServer.call(__MODULE__, {:set_length, seconds})
  @doc "Loops `ids` from 0 s; `[]` means every track with content. Returns :empty if that's none."
  def play(ids), do: GenServer.call(__MODULE__, {:play, ids})
  @doc "Records one pass into track `id` while the other tracks with content play along."
  def record(id) when id in @tracks, do: GenServer.call(__MODULE__, {:record, id})
  def stop, do: GenServer.call(__MODULE__, :stop)
  def clear(ids), do: GenServer.call(__MODULE__, {:clear, ids})
  @doc "The pad's current sound. Kept in the take while recording, ignored otherwise."
  def touch(freq, amp, wave), do: GenServer.cast(__MODULE__, {:touch, freq, amp, wave})
  def status, do: GenServer.call(__MODULE__, :status)

  @doc "The last event at or before `position` ms, or nil before the first one. `events` are sorted by time."
  # ponytail: linear scan per tick, fine for a few thousand events; keep a cursor per track if takes get long.
  def at(events, position), do: events |> Enum.take_while(fn {t, _, _, _} -> t <= position end) |> List.last()

  @impl GenServer
  def init(nil) do
    :timer.send_interval(@tick_ms, :tick)
    {:ok, %{length_ms: 8_000, tracks: Map.new(@tracks, &{&1, []}), mode: :idle, t0: 0, take: [], sent: %{}}}
  end

  @impl GenServer
  def handle_call({:set_length, seconds}, _from, %{mode: :idle} = s),
    do: {:reply, :ok, %{s | length_ms: 1_000 * (seconds |> max(1) |> min(30))}}

  def handle_call({:set_length, _seconds}, _from, s), do: {:reply, :busy, s}

  def handle_call({:play, ids}, _from, s) do
    case if(ids == [], do: filled(s), else: ids) do
      [] -> {:reply, :empty, s}
      ids -> {:reply, :ok, start(s, {:playing, ids})}
    end
  end

  def handle_call({:record, id}, _from, s), do: {:reply, :ok, start(s, {:recording, id, filled(s) -- [id]})}
  def handle_call(:stop, _from, s), do: {:reply, :ok, idle(s)}

  def handle_call({:clear, ids}, _from, s) do
    # Clearing the track being recorded also throws away the take.
    s =
      case s.mode do
        {:recording, id, _} -> if id in ids, do: idle(s), else: s
        _ -> s
      end

    {:reply, :ok, %{s | tracks: Enum.reduce(ids, s.tracks, &Map.put(&2, &1, []))}}
  end

  def handle_call(:status, _from, s) do
    # What the playing tracks sound like right now, so the pad can light their bands.
    sounding = for({_id, {_t, freq, amp, _wave}} <- s.sent, amp > 0, do: {freq, amp}) |> Enum.sort()
    {:reply, %{mode: s.mode, position_ms: position(s), length_ms: s.length_ms, filled: filled(s), sounding: sounding}, s}
  end

  @impl GenServer
  def handle_cast({:touch, freq, amp, wave}, %{mode: {:recording, _, _}} = s),
    do: {:noreply, %{s | take: [{position(s), freq, amp, wave} | s.take]}}

  def handle_cast({:touch, _freq, _amp, _wave}, s), do: {:noreply, s}

  @impl GenServer
  def handle_info(:tick, %{mode: :idle} = s), do: {:noreply, s}

  def handle_info(:tick, %{mode: {:recording, id, _}} = s) do
    if position(s) >= s.length_ms do
      tracks = %{s.tracks | id => Enum.reverse(s.take)}
      {:noreply, %{idle(s) | tracks: tracks}}
    else
      {:noreply, replay(s)}
    end
  end

  def handle_info(:tick, s), do: {:noreply, replay(s)}

  # Sends each playing track's current event to its Synth voice, only when it changed.
  defp replay(s) do
    ids =
      case s.mode do
        {:playing, ids} -> ids
        {:recording, _id, ids} -> ids
      end

    pos = rem(position(s), s.length_ms)

    Enum.reduce(ids, s, fn id, s ->
      event = at(s.tracks[id], pos)
      if event != s.sent[id], do: voice(id, event)
      %{s | sent: Map.put(s.sent, id, event)}
    end)
  end

  defp voice(id, nil), do: Synth.off(id)

  defp voice(id, {_t, freq, amp, wave}) do
    Synth.wave(id, wave)
    Synth.set(id, freq, amp)
  end

  defp start(s, mode), do: %{silence(s) | mode: mode, t0: now(), take: []}
  defp idle(s), do: %{silence(s) | mode: :idle, take: []}

  defp silence(s) do
    Enum.each(@tracks, &Synth.off/1)
    %{s | sent: %{}}
  end

  defp filled(s), do: for({id, events} <- s.tracks, events != [], do: id)

  defp position(%{mode: :idle}), do: 0
  defp position(%{mode: {:recording, _, _}} = s), do: now() - s.t0
  defp position(s), do: rem(now() - s.t0, s.length_ms)

  defp now, do: System.monotonic_time(:millisecond)
end
```

This check records a 1 s take, so it takes about 1.5 s. Run it before
starting the pad: it stops a running sequencer.

```elixir
# Check: Sequencer.at/2 finds the event in effect, and a short take records, plays, restarts and clears.
events = [{100, 220.0, 0.5, :sine}, {300, 440.0, 0.2, :saw}]
nil = Sequencer.at(events, 50)
{100, 220.0, 0.5, :sine} = Sequencer.at(events, 100)
{100, 220.0, 0.5, :sine} = Sequencer.at(events, 299)
{300, 440.0, 0.2, :saw} = Sequencer.at(events, 5_000)
nil = Sequencer.at([], 10)

if Process.whereis(Sequencer), do: GenServer.stop(Sequencer)
{:ok, _} = Sequencer.start()

# Nothing recorded yet: PLAY does nothing.
:empty = Sequencer.play([])

# A one-pass take of 1 s into track 1 ends by itself.
:ok = Sequencer.set_length(1)
:ok = Sequencer.record(1)
Sequencer.touch(440.0, 0.3, :sine)
Process.sleep(200)
Sequencer.touch(440.0, 0.0, :sine)
Process.sleep(1_000)
%{mode: :idle, filled: [1], length_ms: 1_000} = Sequencer.status()

# PLAY with nothing selected plays every track with content; the length can't change meanwhile.
:ok = Sequencer.play([])
%{mode: {:playing, [1]}} = Sequencer.status()
# 50 ms in, track 1 sounds as recorded; its band can light up.
Process.sleep(50)
%{sounding: [{440.0, 0.3}]} = Sequencer.status()
:busy = Sequencer.set_length(5)

# REC while playing restarts in recording mode; STOP throws the unfinished take away.
:ok = Sequencer.record(2)
%{mode: {:recording, 2, [1]}} = Sequencer.status()
Sequencer.touch(300.0, 0.4, :saw)
:ok = Sequencer.stop()
%{mode: :idle, filled: [1]} = Sequencer.status()

# Clearing the track being recorded stops the recording and keeps nothing.
:ok = Sequencer.record(3)
Sequencer.touch(330.0, 0.4, :square)
:ok = Sequencer.clear([3])
%{mode: :idle, filled: [1]} = Sequencer.status()

# Clearing, and the length limits.
:ok = Sequencer.clear([1])
%{filled: []} = Sequencer.status()
:ok = Sequencer.set_length(99)
%{length_ms: 30_000} = Sequencer.status()
:ok = Sequencer.set_length(0)
%{length_ms: 1_000} = Sequencer.status()

GenServer.stop(Sequencer)
```

Start it:

```elixir
{:ok, _} = Sequencer.start()
```

## The pad

The screen spans 4 octaves, from A2 (110 Hz) on the left to A6
(1760 Hz) on the right. Each note gets a band of the same width, dark
for sharps like the black keys on a piano, and every white key is
labelled, with the Cs in white.
The pitch slides smoothly between them, so a note is only in tune in
the middle of its band. The top of the pad is loud and the bottom
is silent.

The bar across the top has two rows. The first shows the note and its
frequency, and `< sine >` arrows that step through the waveforms. The
second runs the sequencer:

* `1` to `4` select tracks; a yellow dot means the track has a take.
* `REC` records one loop into the selected track. It needs exactly one
  selected track and turns red while recording.
* `PLAY` loops the selected tracks, or every track with a take when
  none is selected, until `STOP`.
* `-` and `+` set the loop length, 1 to 30 s, while nothing runs.
* `DEL` clears the selected tracks after a second tap within 2 s.
* The 8 cells on the right show where the loop is.

Touching the bar doesn't play. Bands light up for the tracks playing
back as well as for your finger.

```elixir
defmodule Theremin.Scene do
  use Scenic.Scene
  alias Scenic.Graph
  import Scenic.Primitives

  @low 110
  @octaves 4
  @waves [:sine, :square, :saw]
  @names ~w(A A# B C C# D D# E F F# G G#)
  # Height of the top bar. Row 1: the note and the waveform arrows. Row 2: the sequencer.
  @bar 320
  # Every button is a square this size.
  @button 130
  # Button colours.
  @idle {40, 48, 110}
  @dim {22, 26, 50}
  @selected {90, 110, 230}
  @red {200, 30, 30}
  @green {30, 150, 60}
  # Loop position cells.
  @cell_on {140, 170, 255}
  @cell_off {30, 36, 84}

  @impl Scenic.Scene
  def init(scene, _param, _opts) do
    # Landscape: everything is drawn on a turned canvas, with the volume buttons at the bottom.
    {screen_w, screen_h} = scene.viewport.size
    {width, height} = {screen_h, screen_w}

    graph =
      Graph.build(font: :roboto, font_size: 44)
      |> group(
        fn g ->
          g
          |> rect({width, height}, fill: {20, 24, 60})
          |> note_bands(width, height)
          |> rect({width, @bar}, fill: {10, 12, 30})
          |> text("Slide to play", id: :note, font_size: 80, fill: :white, translate: {40, 105})
          |> text("sine", id: :wave, font_size: 64, fill: :white, text_align: :center, translate: {width - 320, 96})
          |> draw_buttons(width)
          |> text("8 s", id: :length, font_size: 56, fill: :white, text_align: :center, translate: {1410, 255})
          |> progress_cells()
          |> circle(60, id: :dot, fill: :yellow, hidden: true)
        end,
        rotate: -:math.pi() / 2,
        translate: {0, screen_h}
      )

    request_input(scene, [:cursor_button, :cursor_pos, :key])
    # Polls the sequencer, so the bar notices when a recording ends by itself.
    :timer.send_interval(100, :refresh)

    scene =
      assign(scene,
        graph: graph,
        size: {width, height},
        down: false,
        wave: :sine,
        freq: 440.0,
        lit: nil,
        selected: MapSet.new(),
        delete_armed_at: nil,
        status: Sequencer.status(),
        view: nil
      )

    {:noreply, scene} = redraw(scene)
    {:ok, scene}
  end

  # One band per note, centred on its pitch: sharps dark like piano keys, naturals labelled.
  defp note_bands(graph, width, height) do
    notes = 12 * @octaves
    band = width / notes

    Enum.reduce(0..notes, graph, fn n, graph ->
      name = Enum.at(@names, Integer.mod(n, 12))
      x = band * (n - 0.5)
      sharp = String.ends_with?(name, "#")
      graph = rect(graph, {band, height - @bar}, id: {:band, n}, fill: dark(n), stroke: {1, {20, 24, 60}}, translate: {x, @bar})

      cond do
        sharp -> graph
        name == "C" -> label(graph, n, :white, {x + band / 2, height - 30})
        true -> label(graph, n, {150, 160, 230}, {x + band / 2, height - 30})
      end
    end)
  end

  # Unlit bands: sharps darker, like piano keys.
  defp dark(n), do: if(String.ends_with?(Enum.at(@names, Integer.mod(n, 12)), "#"), do: {12, 14, 36}, else: {30, 36, 84})

  # Fire gradient by volume: red when loud (top), through orange, to yellow when quiet (bottom).
  defp fire(t) do
    t = t |> max(0) |> min(1)
    {round(180 + 75 * min(2 * t, 1)), round(220 * t), 0}
  end

  defp paint(graph, n, fill), do: Graph.modify(graph, {:band, n}, &Scenic.Primitive.put_style(&1, :fill, fill))

  defp unlight(graph, nil), do: graph
  defp unlight(graph, n), do: paint(graph, n, dark(n))

  defp label(graph, n, fill, pos),
    do: text(graph, note_name(@low * 2 ** (n / 12)), font_size: 22, fill: fill, text_align: :center, translate: pos)

  # Every button as {id, {x, y}, label}. The same list draws them and hit-tests touches.
  defp buttons(width) do
    tracks = for id <- 1..4, do: {{:track, id}, {track_x(id), 170}, "#{id}"}

    tracks ++
      [
        {:wave_prev, {width - 600, 10}, "<"},
        {:wave_next, {width - 170, 10}, ">"},
        {:rec, {700, 170}, "REC"},
        {:play, {850, 170}, "PLAY"},
        {:stop, {1000, 170}, "STOP"},
        {:shorter, {1200, 170}, "-"},
        {:longer, {1490, 170}, "+"},
        {:delete, {1680, 170}, "DEL"}
      ]
  end

  defp track_x(id), do: 40 + (id - 1) * 150

  defp draw_buttons(graph, width) do
    graph =
      Enum.reduce(buttons(width), graph, fn {id, {x, y}, label}, graph ->
        size = if String.length(label) == 1, do: 72, else: 40

        graph
        |> rect({@button, @button}, id: {:bg, id}, fill: @idle, stroke: {2, {90, 100, 180}}, translate: {x, y})
        |> text(label,
          id: {:label, id},
          font_size: size,
          fill: :white,
          text_align: :center,
          translate: {x + @button / 2, y + @button / 2 + size / 3}
        )
      end)

    # A yellow dot on a track button when the track has content.
    Enum.reduce(1..4, graph, fn id, graph ->
      circle(graph, 10, id: {:filled, id}, fill: :yellow, hidden: true, translate: {track_x(id) + @button - 22, 192})
    end)
  end

  # Loop position: 8 cells that fill up as the loop plays.
  defp progress_cells(graph) do
    Enum.reduce(0..7, graph, fn i, graph -> rect(graph, {30, 60}, id: {:cell, i}, fill: @cell_off, translate: {1840 + i * 35, 205}) end)
  end

  defp button_at(width, {x, y}) do
    Enum.find_value(buttons(width), fn {id, {bx, by}, _label} ->
      if x >= bx and x < bx + @button and y >= by and y < by + @button, do: id
    end)
  end

  @impl Scenic.Scene
  def handle_input({:cursor_button, {_button, 1, _mods, pos}}, _id, scene) do
    {width, _height} = scene.assigns.size
    {_x, y} = pos = canvas(scene, pos)

    cond do
      y >= @bar -> play(assign(scene, down: true), pos)
      id = button_at(width, pos) -> press(scene, id)
      # The rest of the bar doesn't play.
      true -> {:noreply, scene}
    end
  end

  def handle_input({:cursor_button, {_button, 0, _mods, _pos}}, _id, scene) do
    Synth.off(:live)
    # The release goes into the take too, so it has silence where you lifted your finger.
    if scene.assigns.down, do: Sequencer.touch(scene.assigns.freq, 0.0, scene.assigns.wave)

    scene.assigns.graph
    |> unlight(scene.assigns.lit)
    |> echo(scene.assigns.view)
    |> Graph.modify(:dot, &circle(&1, 60, hidden: true))
    |> show(assign(scene, down: false, lit: nil))
  end

  def handle_input({:cursor_pos, pos}, _id, %{assigns: %{down: true}} = scene), do: play(scene, canvas(scene, pos))

  # Only the volume keys: every touch also arrives as a :btn_touch key event.
  def handle_input({:key, {:key_volumeup, 1, _mods}}, _id, scene), do: switch_wave(scene, 1)
  def handle_input({:key, {:key_volumedown, 1, _mods}}, _id, scene), do: switch_wave(scene, -1)

  def handle_input(_input, _id, scene), do: {:noreply, scene}

  @impl GenServer
  def handle_info(:refresh, scene), do: refresh(scene)

  defp press(scene, :wave_prev), do: switch_wave(scene, -1)
  defp press(scene, :wave_next), do: switch_wave(scene, 1)

  defp press(scene, {:track, id}) do
    selected = scene.assigns.selected
    selected = if id in selected, do: MapSet.delete(selected, id), else: MapSet.put(selected, id)
    redraw(assign(scene, selected: selected, delete_armed_at: nil))
  end

  defp press(scene, :rec) do
    # REC needs exactly one selected track.
    with [id] <- MapSet.to_list(scene.assigns.selected), do: Sequencer.record(id)
    refresh(scene)
  end

  defp press(scene, :play) do
    Sequencer.play(Enum.sort(scene.assigns.selected))
    refresh(scene)
  end

  defp press(scene, :stop) do
    Sequencer.stop()
    refresh(scene)
  end

  defp press(scene, :shorter), do: change_length(scene, -1)
  defp press(scene, :longer), do: change_length(scene, 1)

  # DEL needs a second tap within 2 s, and at least one selected track.
  defp press(%{assigns: %{selected: selected, delete_armed_at: armed_at}} = scene, :delete) do
    cond do
      MapSet.size(selected) == 0 ->
        {:noreply, scene}

      armed_at != nil and now() - armed_at <= 2_000 ->
        Sequencer.clear(Enum.sort(selected))
        refresh(assign(scene, delete_armed_at: nil))

      true ->
        redraw(assign(scene, delete_armed_at: now()))
    end
  end

  defp change_length(scene, step) do
    Sequencer.set_length(div(scene.assigns.status.length_ms, 1_000) + step)
    refresh(scene)
  end

  defp refresh(scene), do: redraw(assign(scene, status: Sequencer.status()))

  # Pushes a new graph only when what the bar shows has changed.
  defp redraw(scene) do
    view = view(scene.assigns)

    if view == scene.assigns.view do
      {:noreply, scene}
    else
      scene.assigns.graph |> apply_view(view, scene.assigns) |> show(assign(scene, view: view))
    end
  end

  # What the bar should show, from the selection and the sequencer's status.
  defp view(%{selected: selected, status: status, delete_armed_at: armed_at}) do
    armed = armed_at != nil and now() - armed_at <= 2_000
    idle = status.mode == :idle

    rec =
      cond do
        match?({:recording, _, _}, status.mode) -> @red
        MapSet.size(selected) == 1 -> @idle
        true -> @dim
      end

    delete =
      cond do
        armed -> @red
        MapSet.size(selected) == 0 -> @dim
        true -> @idle
      end

    length = if idle, do: @idle, else: @dim

    fills =
      Map.new(1..4, &{{:track, &1}, if(&1 in selected, do: @selected, else: @idle)})
      |> Map.merge(%{
        rec: rec,
        play: if(match?({:playing, _}, status.mode), do: @green, else: @idle),
        shorter: length,
        longer: length,
        delete: delete
      })

    %{
      fills: fills,
      filled: status.filled,
      lit: if(idle, do: 0, else: min(div(8 * status.position_ms, status.length_ms) + 1, 8)),
      length: "#{div(status.length_ms, 1_000)} s",
      # Bands lit by playback: note index => fire colour of its volume.
      echo: Map.new(status.sounding, fn {freq, amp} -> {round(12 * :math.log2(freq / @low)), fire(1 - amp / 0.5)} end),
      delete: if(armed, do: "again?", else: "DEL")
    }
  end

  defp apply_view(graph, view, %{view: old, lit: live}) do
    # Playback bands that went quiet go dark, unless the finger is on them.
    old_echo = if old, do: Map.keys(old.echo), else: []
    graph = Enum.reduce((old_echo -- Map.keys(view.echo)) -- [live], graph, &unlight(&2, &1))
    graph = echo(graph, view)

    graph =
      Enum.reduce(view.fills, graph, fn {id, fill}, graph ->
        Graph.modify(graph, {:bg, id}, &Scenic.Primitive.put_style(&1, :fill, fill))
      end)

    graph =
      Enum.reduce(1..4, graph, fn id, graph ->
        Graph.modify(graph, {:filled, id}, &Scenic.Primitive.put_style(&1, :hidden, id not in view.filled))
      end)

    graph =
      Enum.reduce(0..7, graph, fn i, graph ->
        Graph.modify(graph, {:cell, i}, &Scenic.Primitive.put_style(&1, :fill, if(i < view.lit, do: @cell_on, else: @cell_off)))
      end)

    graph
    |> Graph.modify({:label, :delete}, &text(&1, view.delete))
    |> Graph.modify(:length, &text(&1, view.length))
  end

  defp echo(graph, nil), do: graph
  defp echo(graph, view), do: Enum.reduce(view.echo, graph, fn {n, fill}, graph -> paint(graph, n, fill) end)

  # Step through @waves, wrapping around at both ends.
  defp switch_wave(scene, step) do
    wave = Enum.at(@waves, Integer.mod(Enum.find_index(@waves, &(&1 == scene.assigns.wave)) + step, length(@waves)))
    Synth.wave(:live, wave)

    scene.assigns.graph
    |> Graph.modify(:wave, &text(&1, "#{wave}"))
    |> show(assign(scene, wave: wave))
  end

  # Touches arrive in portrait screen coordinates: turn them back into the canvas.
  defp canvas(%{assigns: %{size: {width, _height}}}, {screen_x, screen_y}), do: {width - screen_y, screen_x}

  defp play(scene, {x, y}) do
    {width, height} = scene.assigns.size
    # Dragging into the bar keeps playing at full volume.
    y = max(y, @bar)
    quiet = (y - @bar) / (height - @bar)
    freq = @low * 2 ** (@octaves * x / width)
    amp = 0.5 * (1 - quiet)
    Synth.set(:live, freq, amp)
    Sequencer.touch(freq, amp, scene.assigns.wave)
    lit = round(12 * @octaves * x / width)

    # Recoloured on every move: the colour follows the volume.
    scene.assigns.graph
    |> then(&if(scene.assigns.lit == lit, do: &1, else: &1 |> unlight(scene.assigns.lit) |> echo(scene.assigns.view)))
    |> paint(lit, fire(quiet))
    |> Graph.modify(:dot, &circle(&1, 60, translate: {x, y}, hidden: false))
    |> Graph.modify(:note, &text(&1, "#{note_name(freq)}  #{round(freq)} Hz"))
    |> show(assign(scene, lit: lit, freq: freq))
  end

  # The nearest note, counted in semitones from A4 (440 Hz).
  defp note_name(freq) do
    n = round(12 * :math.log2(freq / 440))
    "#{Enum.at(@names, Integer.mod(n, 12))}#{4 + Integer.floor_div(n + 9, 12)}"
  end

  defp now, do: System.monotonic_time(:millisecond)

  defp show(graph, scene), do: {:noreply, scene |> assign(graph: graph) |> push_graph(graph)}
end
```

## Play

Detach the IEx console from the screen, then start the pad. The synth
and the sequencer have to be running (see above).

The screen is 1080 × 2160 in portrait, and the driver only draws in
portrait. So the scene draws on a 2160 × 1080 canvas and turns it a
quarter turn onto the screen, and turns each touch back the other way.
Hold the phone with the volume buttons at the bottom.

```elixir
fbcon =
  Path.wildcard("/sys/class/vtconsole/vtcon*")
  |> Enum.find(&(File.read!(Path.join(&1, "name")) =~ "frame buffer"))

File.write!(Path.join(fbcon, "bind"), "0")
File.write!("/sys/class/graphics/fb0/blank", "0")

{:ok, viewport} =
  Scenic.ViewPort.start(
    name: :fp3_screen,
    size: {1080, 2160},
    default_scene: Theremin.Scene,
    # cursor: true makes the driver report finger movement, not just touch and release.
    drivers: [[module: Scenic.Driver.Local, cursor: true]]
  )
```

Try these:

* Change `@low` or `@octaves` in the scene, run **Stop**, then run
  the scene and **Play** cells again.
* Add a waveform of your own to `shape/2` in `Synth`.
* Record a bass line on track 1, then a melody on track 2 while it plays.
* Replace the volume with the phone's tilt from the
  [Sensors](03_sensors.livemd) notebook.

## Stop

```elixir
Scenic.ViewPort.stop(viewport)
GenServer.stop(Sequencer)
Synth.stop()
File.write!(Path.join(fbcon, "bind"), "1")
```
